{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000ca75","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c45a-c3aa-73a1-9c84-6d5fc23e6787"},"sequence":917,"recorded_at":1780531400001829,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"892d0e23-8edf-4e7b-8430-1bc89cf9b07d","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"892d0e23-8edf-4e7b-8430-1bc89cf9b07d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"892d0e23-8edf-4e7b-8430-1bc89cf9b07d"},"call_id":"call_01a0c45d23da777eaa2baf7ed5303b5c","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/nofollow.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"errors\"\n5|\t\"os\"\n6|\t\"strings\"\n7|)\n8|\n9|// errIsSymlink reports the Windows Lstat gate refusal. It stays internal:\n10|// callers see the failPath refusal, and errors.Is against ErrUnsafePath\n11|// is the supported match.\n12|var errIsSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink refused before open\")\n13|\n14|// errCommitSymlink is the internal signal from the platform commit walk\n15|// (openCommitRelative) that a symlink or reparse point stands on the\n16|// member path at commit time. It stays internal: Open maps it to the\n17|// failPath \"member symlink escape\" refusal, and errors.Is against\n18|// ErrUnsafePath is the supported match. The walk returns no failPath\n19|// sites of its own so the refusal inventory stays in shared code,\n20|// which the suite executes on every platform it runs on.\n21|var errCommitSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink on the commit path\")\n22|\n23|// guardRootMatches reports whether the open directory handle refers to\n24|// the same directory as rootPath: the handle's fstat identity against a\n25|// stat of the path (device and inode on unix via os.SameFile, volume\n26|// and file index on Windows). A stat failure on either side is not a\n27|// match: an unreadable root fails closed into the mismatch refusal\n28|// rather than admitting an unverifiable handle.\n29|func guardRootMatches(root *os.File, rootPath string) bool {\n30|\thandleInfo, err := root.Stat()\n31|\tif err != nil || handleInfo == nil {\n32|\t\treturn false\n33|\t}\n34|\tpathInfo, err := os.Stat(rootPath)\n35|\tif err != nil || pathInfo == nil {\n36|\t\treturn false\n37|\t}\n38|\treturn os.SameFile(handleInfo, pathInfo)\n39|}\n40|\n41|// OpenNoFollowFile opens an existing regular file read-only, refusing a\n42|// trailing symlink instead of traversing it (Section 16.3\n43|// \"symlink/reparse escape during both validation and commit\"). The\n44|// descriptor refers to the opened inode, and the post-open Fstat below\n45|// reads that descriptor rather than the path, so a swap between open and\n46|// stat cannot retarget the check: only the final-component open itself\n47|// races, and on unix O_NOFOLLOW closes even that.\n48|//\n49|// The file must be regular: directories, devices, pipes, and sockets are\n50|// refused after opening (and the descriptor closed) rather than handed\n51|// back. Parent directories are the caller's responsibility: open the\n52|// staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and commit members through\n53|// Guard.Open, which binds the walk to the root handle. Composing\n54|// Guard.Resolve with this function instead is not contained: Resolve\n55|// answers the lexical question only, and a symlinked intermediate\n56|// directory redirects a path-string open outside the root.\n57|func OpenNoFollowFile(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n58|\tfile, err := openNoFollow(path)\n59|\tif err != nil {\n60|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n61|\t}\n62|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(file)\n63|\tif err != nil {\n64|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n65|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n66|\t}\n67|\tif err := CheckRegularTarget(info); err != nil {\n68|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n69|\t\treturn nil, err\n70|\t}\n71|\t// The unix open sets O_NONBLOCK so a FIFO can never stall the opener\n72|\t// (its refusal above runs only after the open returns). A regular\n73|\t// file ignores O_NONBLOCK on reads, and clearing it here keeps a\n74|\t// borrowed descriptor from leaking the flag to its reader. fcntl on\n75|\t// a just-opened valid descriptor cannot fail; the best-effort clear\n76|\t// carries no refusal site for that reason.\n77|\t_ = clearNonblock(file)\n78|\treturn file, nil\n79|}\n80|\n81|// Open commits one guard member to an open regular file through the\n82|// staging-root directory handle, refusing symlink and reparse escape at\n83|// commit time (Section 16.3 \"symlink/reparse escape during both\n84|// validation and commit\").\n85|//\n86|// Validation and commit are two halves of one entry point, not two calls\n87|// the caller composes: the member grammar and the managed set refuse\n88|// lexically first, then the platform walk (openCommitRelative) opens\n89|// every path component relative to a directory descriptor — never by\n90|// re-resolving a path string — so a symlinked intermediate directory\n91|// cannot redirect the commit outside the root the way composing\n92|// Guard.Resolve with OpenNoFollowFile does (O_NOFOLLOW constrains only\n93|// the final component; every component before it is still followed by\n94|// the kernel when a path string is opened).\n95|//\n96|// The caller opens the staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and passes the\n97|// handle: on unix the walk never leaves that handle (openat from the\n98|// root descriptor down, O_NOFOLLOW per component, TOCTOU-closed); on\n99|// Windows the walk is a best-effort Lstat component check with the\n100|// stated check-then-open bound. The returned file is cleared of\n101|// O_NONBLOCK like OpenNoFollowFile. The caller owns closing both the\n102|// root handle and the returned file.\n103|//\n104|// The handle is bound to the guard root before descending:\n105|// guardRootMatches fstats the handle and compares device and inode\n106|// against a stat of guard.root, refusing a foreign directory handle\n107|// with \"guard root mismatch\". The check lives here in shared code so\n108|// both platforms answer the mismatch identically; past it, the unix\n109|// walk descends from the verified handle and the Windows walk from the\n110|// verified root path, which name the same directory.\n111|func (guard Guard) Open(root *os.File, member string) (*os.File, error) {\n112|\tif err := CheckMemberPath(guard.platform, member); err != nil {\n113|\t\treturn nil, err\n114|\t}\n115|\tif guard.checkManaged && !guard.managed[member] {\n116|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"member unmanaged\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n117|\t}\n118|\tif root == nil {\n119|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"guard root handle\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n120|\t}\n121|\tif info, err := root.Stat(); err != nil || info == nil || !info.IsDir() {\n122|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"guard root handle\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n123|\t}\n124|\tif !guardRootMatches(root, guard.root) {\n125|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"guard root mismatch\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n126|\t}\n127|\tsegments := strings.Split(member, \"/\")\n128|\tfile, err := openCommitRelative(root, guard.root, member, segments)\n129|\tif err != nil {\n130|\t\tif errors.Is(err, errCommitSymlink) {\n131|\t\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"member symlink escape\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n132|\t\t}\n133|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(member)).WithCause(err)\n134|\t}\n135|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(file)\n136|\tif err != nil {\n137|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n138|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(member)).WithCause(err)\n139|\t}\n140|\tif err := CheckRegularTarget(info); err != nil {\n141|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n142|\t\treturn nil, err\n143|\t}\n144|\t_ = clearNonblock(file)\n145|\treturn file, nil\n146|}\n147|\n148|// statOpenedFile stats an already-opened descriptor. It is a variable\n149|// only so seam-failure tests can force the stat-failure path through the\n150|// production openers below: fstat on a live descriptor does not fail on\n151|// any supported platform, and without the seam the error branch is\n152|// untestable. Production code never reassigns it.\n153|var statOpenedFile = func(file *os.File) (os.FileInfo, error) {\n154|\treturn file.Stat()\n155|}\n156|\n157|// OpenNoFollowDir opens an existing directory read-only without following\n158|// a trailing symlink, returning a handle for validation walks. The handle\n159|// pins the opened directory: Fstat and ReadDirnames on it observe the\n160|// directory that was opened even if its name is later retargeted.\n161|//\n162|// The Lstat fast path refuses non-directories with the same rules on\n163|// every platform; the post-open Stat below stays authoritative against a\n164|// swap between the check and the open.\n165|func OpenNoFollowDir(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n166|\tif info, err := os.Lstat(path); err != nil {\n167|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n168|\t} else if info.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n169|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target symlink\", info.Name())\n170|\t} else if !info.IsDir() {\n171|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target directory\", info.Name())\n172|\t}\n173|\tdir, err := openNoFollowDir(path)\n174|\tif err != nil {\n175|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n176|\t}\n177|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(dir)\n178|\tif err != nil {\n179|\t\t_ = dir.Close()\n180|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n181|\t}\n182|\tif info == nil || !info.IsDir() || info.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n183|\t\t_ = dir.Close()\n184|\t\tname := path\n185|\t\tif info != nil {\n186|\t\t\tname = info.Name()\n187|\t\t}\n188|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target directory\", name)\n189|\t}\n190|\treturn dir, nil\n191|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
